        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$R(x)=5x^3-5x=5(x-1)x(x+1)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\nR(x)&=x^5-x-Q(x)\\\\\nR(x)&=x^5-x-(x^5-5x^3+4x)\\\\\nR(x)&=5x^3-5x\\\\\nR(x)&=5(x-1)x(x+1).\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q50-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that $n^5-n$ is always divisible by $30$ for any integer $n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n^5-n=Q(n)+R(n)$ is divisible by $30$.\n\n$Q(n)$ contains five consecutive integer factors, so is divisible by $2$, $3$ and $5$. Also, $R(n)=5(n-1)n(n+1)$ is $5$ times a multiple of $6$.",
          "worked": "$$Q(n)=(n-2)(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2).$$\nAmong five consecutive integers there is a multiple of $2$, a multiple of $3$ and a multiple of $5$. Since $2$, $3$ and $5$ are pairwise coprime, $Q(n)$ is divisible by $30$.\n\n$$R(n)=5(n-1)n(n+1).$$\nAmong three consecutive integers there is an even integer and a multiple of $3$. Their product is therefore divisible by $6$, so $R(n)$ is divisible by $30$.\n\nSince $n^5-n=Q(n)+R(n)$, it is divisible by $30$ for every integer $n$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
